WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Part 4. You will hear a literature student giving a talk about Victor Hugo, a famous 19th century French writer.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:45.000
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:18.000
The person I've chosen to talk about is the French writer Victor Hugo.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Many people have heard of him because his novel Les Miserables, which he wrote in 1862, is famous around the world.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
It became a stage musical in the 1980s and a film version was also released in 2012.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.000
So some of us, I'm sure, have a pretty general idea of the plot, but we know much less about the author.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Today I'm going to provide a little more insight into this talented man

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:57.000
and I'm going to talk particularly about the home he had on the island of Guernsey in the British Channel Islands.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.000
But first, his early career. As I've said, he was a writer.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.000
He was at the height of his career in Paris and he was very highly regarded by his colleagues.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
As far as literature was concerned, he was the leading figure of the Romantic movement.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:25.000
However, as well as being a literary genius, he also gave many speeches about issues like the level of poverty in his society.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:33.000
He felt very strongly about this and about other areas where change was needed, like education.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:39.000
This kind of outspoken criticism was not well liked by the rulers of France

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.000
and eventually the Emperor, Napoleon III, told Victor Hugo to leave Paris and not return.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
In other words, he sent him into exile.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
So Victor Hugo was forced to reside in other parts of Europe.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Guernsey was actually his third place of exile and he landed there in 1855.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.000
He produced a lot while on Guernsey, including Les Miserables,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.000
and to do this he had to spend a great deal of time in the home that he had there.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:24.000
This was a property that he bought using the money he'd made in France from the publication of a collection of his poetry.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
It was the only property he ever owned and he was very proud of it.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:39.000
The property Victor Hugo bought on Guernsey was a large five-storey house in the capital town of St Peterport

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:47.000
and he lived there for 15 years, returning to France in 1870 when Napoleon's empire collapsed.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:55.000
He decorated and furnished each level or floor of the house in unique and wonderful ways

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.000
and many people consider the inside of the house to be a work of art.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Today it's a museum that attracts 200,000 visitors a year.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
He lived in the house with his family and portraits of its members still hang in rooms on the ground floor

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.000
along with drawings that he did during his travels that he felt were important to him.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.000
In other ground floor rooms there are huge tapestries that he would have designed and loved.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.000
The walls are covered in dark wood panelling that Victor Hugo created himself

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.000
using wooden furniture that he bought in the market.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:46.000
The items were relatively inexpensive and he used them to create intricate carvings.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.000
They gave an atmosphere on the lower level that was shadowy and rather solemn.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
On the next level of the house there are two impressive lounges where he entertained his guests.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:10.000
One lounge has entirely red furnishings such as sofas and wall coverings and the other blue.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:18.000
There's a strong Chinese influence in these areas in things like the wallpaper pattern and the lamps

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.000
which he would have made himself by copying original versions.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:31.000
His library where he left many of his favourite books forms the hallway to the third floor

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:37.000
and was a comfortable area where he could relax and enjoy his afternoons.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:43.000
And then at the very top of the house there's a room called the lookout.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.000
Called that because it looks out over the harbour.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:54.000
In contrast to the rather dark lower levels it's full of light and was like a glass office

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.000
where he would write until lunchtime, often at his desk.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:06.000
So Victor Hugo was a man of many talents but he was also true to his values.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.000
While living in his house on Guernsey he entertained many other famous writers

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.000
but he also invited a large group of local children from the deprived areas of the island

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.000
to dinner once a week.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:26.000
What's more he served them their food which was an extraordinary treat.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:34.000
In 1927 the house was owned by his relatives and they decided to donate it to the city of Paris.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.000
It has since been restored using photographs from the period

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:46.000
and, as I mentioned earlier, is now a museum that is open to the public.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:53.000
And, as I mentioned earlier, is now a museum that is open to the public.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Thank you for watching.

